{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:69800e0ec50a05a6f91ba7f4e6df1ca4e9cad6e8c5f67f28ce0af4f02e1b1d61","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.33,"B":0.38,"C":0.12,"D":0.17}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Applied the lexical-priority lock mechanically against MY OWN CQ3 and my own OECD core distribution. My CQ3: P10 0.90 / P50 2.25 / P90 3.40, so right sigma = (3.40-2.25)/1.282 = 0.897 and left sigma = (2.25-0.90)/1.282 = 1.053. My OECD-38 GDP-weighted core for the 2032-36 SUB-window: central 2.15%, sigma 0.55 (the same object that produced CQ2's 2.335% ten-year average - the 2030-32 leg at 2.20 and the 2033-36 leg at 2.05 blend to ~2.11, carried at 2.15). STEP 1, A if growth >= 2.6%: P(z >= (2.6-2.25)/0.897) = P(z >= 0.390) = 0.348. I report 33, haircutting ~1.5pp for resolution risk on the growth measure itself (PPP weighting convention; the Chinese deflator). STEP 2, else C if core >= 2.7 AND growth < 2.0: P(core >= 2.7) = P(z >= (2.7-2.15)/0.55) = P(z >= 1.000) = 0.159. P(growth < 2.0) = P(z <= (2.0-2.25)/1.053) = P(z <= -0.237) = 0.406. Positively correlated through the supply-wall mechanism, rho = +0.30: joint = 0.159(0.406) + 0.30*sqrt(0.159*0.841*0.406*0.594) = 0.0646 + 0.30(0.1795) = 0.1185 -> 12. STEP 3, else D if growth < 1.7 AND core < 2.0: P(growth < 1.7) = P(z <= (1.7-2.25)/1.053) = P(z <= -0.522) = 0.301. P(core < 2.0) = P(z <= (2.0-2.15)/0.55) = P(z <= -0.273) = 0.393. rho = +0.25 (the BIS Box C demand-deficit mechanism couples them; the supply-wall story would decouple them): joint = 0.301(0.393) + 0.25*sqrt(0.301*0.699*0.393*0.607) = 0.1183 + 0.25(0.2238) = 0.1743 -> 17. Disjoint from C on inflation and from A on growth. STEP 4, B = residual = 100 - 33 - 12 - 17 = 38. Sum exactly 100. CONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED, as required. (i) Against my own CQ3: A at 33 is exactly P(g >= 2.6) recomputed from my stated P50 and P90 under a normal with sigma 0.897, less a 1.5pp measurement haircut - a reader who recomputes from my P10/P50/P90 must get 34-35. My P50 of 2.25 sits in B's literal band and B is still modal, so I have not violated the lock's prohibition. (ii) Against my own CQ2: CQ2's conjunct A put P(2027-36 core >= 2.50) at 0.37 off a 2.335 central; C needs the tighter 2032-36 core >= 2.70 off a 2.15 central, which is 0.159 - strictly and substantially lower, as the higher threshold (+35bp) and lower sub-window central (-18bp) require. My low CQ2 of 16 is driven by the CHINA conjunct, not the OECD one, so it does not force C down further than this. (iii) Residual-cell audit: B at 38 comprises ~36.6pp of mass in growth 1.7-2.6 that is not captured by A or C, minus the ~11pp of that band that C and the growth-2.0-2.6-with-core>=2.7 cell claim, plus the ~12pp leftover cells the lock dumps into B (growth < 1.7 with core 2.0-2.7). SUBSTANTIVE SHAPE, AND IT IS THE POINT: my distribution is BIMODAL - A at 33 and D at 17 both above the Wave-1 panel (13-19 and 17-20), with B thinned from their 44-49 to 38. That is the direct consequence of taking their own 66-80% bust probability seriously instead of leaving it in a P10: the coupled world either gets the productivity and the cheap post-bust capital (A) or gets the demand shortfall and the stranded capacity (D), and the elegant barbell with steep real term premia and coexisting scarcity rents is the state that requires the bust to be permanently deferred. Wave 1's modal B is narrative coherence, not probability mass. C at 12 is well below all three because my inflation central for 2032-36 is lower than theirs - a high-productivity, low-real-rate back half with China's goods disinflation only partly spent is not a 2.7% core world.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1